"Operation “No Bell”: Coordinated criticism of the US and allies | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-no-bell/)\nciteturn31448view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-no-bell/\",\"lineno\":24}); Total lines: 155\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: February 1, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Safety L16: # Operation “No Bell”: Coordinated criticism of the US and allies\nL17: \nL18: OpenAI banned accounts linked to a previously unreported, likely Russia-origin operation we dubbed \"No Bell\", using AI to produce criticism of the US and its allies for audiences across Africa.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: This case study was originally published in OpenAI’s cite4†February 2026⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com report on disrupting malicious uses of AI.\nL25: ## Actor\nL26: \nL27: We banned a ChatGPT account that was generating long-form articles and social media content about geopolitics in Africa. We began investigating this operation following a lead from Meta. The account likely originated in Russia. One of the long-form articles advocated that the president of Angola should win the Nobel Peace Prize; the ChatGPT user stated explicitly that this was intended to antagonize the team of US President Donald Trump. Based on this, we have dubbed it “No Bell”.\nL28: ## Behavior\nL29: \nL30: The ChatGPT account’s main activity was generating social media posts and long-form commentary articles about geopolitics in sub-Saharan Africa. The user mainly prompted in English, but sometimes input Russian-language instructions that they attributed to their manager. These instructions included edits and detailed feedback on some of the articles.\nL31: Some of the user’s content was posted on Facebook Pages that posed as news outlets in African countries including South Africa, Ghana, Kenya and Angola. Meta banned these Pages. Under Facebook’s transparency settings, a few of the Pages showed their admin locations, which included Russia and Ukraine.\nL32: One Page focused on Kenya showed that it had originally been called “Farmtown5” and had then changed its name to “Kenya Watchtower”, suggesting that it had been acquired and repurposed from a supplier unrelated to the operation. We also identified references to previously removed Pages targeting Zambia and Namibia.\nL33: Transparency settings for one of the Facebook Pages in the Transparency settings for the Page “Kenya Watchtower”, network, showing the admin locations. The ChatGPT user generated showing the name change. The ChatGPT user generated content that was posted by this Page. Meta banned this Page content that was posted by this Page. Meta banned this Page.\nL34: Transparency settings for one of the Facebook Pages in the network, showing the admin locations. The ChatGPT user generated content that was posted by this Page. Meta banned this Page.\nL35: \nL36: Transparency settings for the Page “Kenya Watchtower”, showing the name change. The ChatGPT user generated content that was posted by this Page. Meta banned this Page.\nL37: The long-form articles were published on a range of African news websites. Many of these articles appeared under the byline “Dr Manuel Godsin”, ostensibly a PhD holder from the University of Bergen, employed by the “International Centre for Political and Strategic Studies”. However, according to one cite5†online report⁠(opens in a new window)†www.africa-confidential.com , no such person exists.\nL38: We searched for references to Manuel Godsin in the Norwegian National Research Information Repository (NVA) and the Bergen University Library, but found no results. Further online research identified web articles that purported to show Godsin’s photo; the same picture featured on the profile of a St Petersburg law student on a Russian legal networking site, apparently posted online in the 2010s.\nL39: Other than finding Godsin’s byline on cite6†53 articles online⁠(opens in a new window)†muckrack.com , we were unable to identify credible evidence of his existence.\nL40: As with operation cite7†“Trolling Stone” , and some of the operations we reported on in cite8†October⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com , this user occasionally prompted the model to generate text without em-dashes, to help conceal the fact that it was AI-generated. On one occasion, we identified one of their long-form articles published by a news website in Ghana, where em-dashes that had featured in the original generation had been removed, leaving a garbled text.\nL41: They also asked our model to make sure its writing was in the style of a human journalist, likely to reduce the appearance of AI-generated content.\nL42: Top, screenshot of the author bio for Dr Manuel Godsin, listing his PhD from the University of Bergen. The article was published by the Sunday Independent, South Africa. Bottom, search results for the name “Manuel Godsin” on the NVA website. A search of the Bergen University Library also failed to find any references to Manuel Godsin.\nL43: Lede of an article generated by this operator and published on a Ghanaian website. In the highlighted sentence, the ChatGPT original featured an em-dash after the word “ceasefire”.\nL44: \nL45: The user also appears to have experimented with different LLMs. On some occasions, they asked the model for image prompts for Gemini; on another, they asked the model to modify an image that appeared to have been generated by Gemini. That image was then posted as the banner of another Facebook Page focused on Kenya.\nL46: ## Completion\nL47: \nL48: The content that this user generated primarily focused on geopolitical themes in sub-Saharan Africa. It typically praised Russia and criticized Ukraine, the United Kingdom and the United States. A few articles were more personal, and focussed on Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskiy or US President Donald Trump.\nL49: Alongside topics of large-scale geopolitics, the user also generated articles about more localized issues. For example, three articles either generated by the operation or published under the Godsin byline focused on German arms manufacturer Rheinmetall, accusing it of using a South African subsidiary to circumvent arms export controls. Another article under the Godsin byline accused British NGO Crisis Action of fomenting protests in South Africa.\nL50: Some prompts focused on generating content about court cases involving British soldiers in Kenya.\nL51: Two headlines on articles generated by this operation and published online.\nL52: \nL53: In parallel, other articles praised or defended Russia and its role in Africa. For example, one article published under the Manuel Godsin byline praised Russia’s presence in the Central African Republic. Ironically, a second article, and accompanying Facebook posts generated by the operation, accused Western leaders of targeting South Africa with disinformation.\nL54: ## Impact\nL55: \nL56: As with the Argentina-focused network described above, this operation achieved little impact on social media. One of its Facebook Pages counted some 3,000 followers before Meta took it down; three of the others, newly created, had almost none. It had more success planting its articles on mainstream news websites, where the “Manuel Godsin” pieces were published.\nL57: Using the IO impact cite9†Breakout Scale⁠(opens in a new window)†www.brookings.edu , which rates IO on a scale of 1 (lowest) to 6 (highest), we would assess this as being towards the low end of Category 4 (breakout to mainstream media), based on the placement of some of its articles in African news sites.\nL58: \nL59:   * cite10†Russia L60:   * cite11†South Africa L61:   * cite12†Ghana L62:   * cite13†Kenya L63:   * cite14†Angola L64:   * cite15†Zambia L65:   * cite16†Namibia L66:   * cite17†Influence operations L67: ## Author\nL68: \nL69: OpenAI\nL70: \nL71: Research\nL72: \nL73:   * cite18†Research Index L74:   * cite19†Research Overview L75:   * cite20†Economic Research L76: \nL77: Latest Advancements\nL78: \nL79:   * cite21†GPT-6.1 Sol L80:   * cite22†GPT-6 Astra L81:   * cite23†GPT-5.6 L82:   * cite24†GPT-5.5 L83: \nL84: Safety\nL85: \nL86:   * cite25†Safety Approach L87:   * cite26†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L88:   * cite27†Security & Privacy L89:   * cite28†Trust & Transparency L90: \nL91: Products\nL92:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L93:   * cite29†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L94:   * cite30†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L95:   * cite31†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L96:   * cite32†Codex†chatgpt.com L97:   * cite33†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L98:   * cite34†Release Notes L99: \nL100: API Platform\nL101: \nL102:   * cite35†Overview L103:   * cite36†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L104:   * cite37†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L105: \nL106: Business\nL107:   * cite38†Overview L108:   * cite39†Solutions L109:   * cite40†Resources L110:   * cite41†Plugins L111:   * cite42†Customer Stories L112:   * cite43†Partner Network L113:   * cite44†Contact Sales L114: \nL115: Developers\nL116: \nL117:   * cite45†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L118:   * cite46†Open Models L119:   * cite47†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L120:   * cite48†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L121:   * cite49†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L122: \nL123: Company\nL124:   * cite50†About Us L125:   * cite51†Our Charter L126:   * cite52†Careers L127:   * cite53†News L128: \nL129: Support\nL130: \nL131:   * cite54†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L132:   * cite55†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L133: \nL134: More\nL135: \nL136:   * cite56†Stories L137:   * cite57†Academy L138:   * cite58†Supply Co. L139:   * cite59†Livestreams L140:   * cite60†Podcast L141:   * cite61†RSS L142: \nL143: Terms & Policies\nL144: \nL145:   * cite62†Terms of Use L146:   * cite63†Privacy Policy L147:   * cite64†Other Policies L148: cite65†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite67†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite68†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite69†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite70†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite71†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L149: \nL150: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL151: \nL152: English United States\nL153: \nL154: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRomance scam: AI-enabled romance scam workflows | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-romance-scam/)\nciteturn31448view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-romance-scam/\",\"lineno\":24}); Total lines: 139\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: February 1, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Safety L16: # Romance scams: AI-enabled romance scam workflows\nL17: \nL18: OpenAI banned accounts using AI to support romance scam workflows, including outreach, translation, victim engagement, and investment-fraud lures.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: This case study was originally published in OpenAI’s cite4†February 2026⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com report on disrupting malicious uses of AI.\nL25: Since we began reporting on our disruptions of scam networks that sought to abuse our models cite5†a year ago⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com , we’ve taken down many scam operations from different parts of the world. They included “task” scams, which defraud their victims by convincing them to pay money in as a way of accessing non-existent earnings for trivial tasks, and investment scams, which defraud their victims by convincing them to put money into non-existent investment companies.\nL26: One common type of scam since long before the days of AI is the romance scam, in which scammers pose as a potential romantic partner and attempt to convince their target that they have met a love match before asking them for ever more money. Our first published scam disruption featured a newly stood up criminal operation in Cambodia that had used ChatGPT to generate messages for a romance scam, some of which were spread on social media. Since then, we’ve disrupted various attempted romance scams.\nL27: As we wrote in cite6†June⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com , these and other scams tend to follow a common pattern in their use of AI, which we think of as the ping (cold contact), the zing (generate emotion), and the sting (extract money).\nL28: The ping (cold contact): the scammer generates content designed to attract the potential target’s attention by appealing to their interests. For example, the “pig butchering” scam we reported last year frequently targeted American men in their 40s in the medical professions by replying to social media posts they made about golf. Other operations used cold-call SMS messages, fake recruitment messages or, in our most recent romance scam case (described below), social media ads.\nL29: In each case, the threat actors used ChatGPT to generate messages that might be more engaging and less obviously non-native than traditional scams.\nL30: Golf-themed ping from the “pig butchering” network we reported in 2025, replying to a Facebook post by a user not linked to the operation.\nL31: The zing (generate emotion): the scammer generates content designed to trigger strong emotions in the target, and thus make them easier to manipulate. Romance scams try to make the target attracted to the scammer. Other scams can include trying to make the target excited about a potentially lucrative deal, afraid of missing an opportunity, or alarmed about an alleged legal risk or unpaid bill. Sometimes, the zing can be included in the same message as the ping.\nL32: Cold-call SMS from the scam operation “Wrong Number” that we exposed in June 2025, including details of implausibly high returns for little work, likely designed to create the “zing” effect.\nL33: The sting (extract money): the scammer generates content designed to convince the target to hand over money. The reasons given can vary enormously. For example, romance scams may ask the target to invest in the beloved’s business, or hand over money to cover a financial crisis. “Task” scams tell their targets to pay money in so they can access (fictional) money they believe they earned. Investment scams tell the targets to put their money into non-existent investments.\nL34: Cold-call SMS from the scam operation “Wrong Number” that we exposed in June 2025, including details of implausibly high returns for little work, likely designed to create the “zing” effect.\nL35: As this flow suggests, an essential component of scams is the distribution network.\nL36: Different scam operations that we’ve exposed sent their pings via SMS, encrypted messaging apps, social media posts, online ads, or a combination of all of them; scams in the pre-AI era are notorious for having used cite7†emails⁠(opens in a new window)†consumer.ftc.gov , cite8†phone calls⁠(opens in a new window)†consumer.ftc.gov , or even, in the nineteenth century, cite9†letters⁠(opens in a new window)†www.laphamsquarterly.org and cite10†telegrams⁠(opens in a new window)†www.aobf.org .\nL37: Many scams take a scattergun approach to distribution, but some appear to attempt precision targeting. For example, the “pig butchering” scam cite5†we reported in February 2025⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com focused on topics such as golf; another romance scam used social media ads to target young men in Indonesia; and cite11†celebrity scams⁠(opens in a new window)†consumer.ftc.gov typically pose as a famous person and then target that person’s fan groups.\nL38: While the fragmentary nature of the evidence makes it difficult to reliably compare different scams that we disrupted, we assess that the scam’s chosen distribution method (e.g., scattershot or targeted) plays a significant role in each scam’s ability to successfully reach and exploit its targets, regardless of the degree to which the operation used AI for different functions.\nL39:   * cite12†Cambodia L40:   * cite13†United States L41:   * cite14†Indonesia L42:   * cite15†Fraud & scams L43: ## Author\nL44: \nL45: OpenAI\nL46: ## Keep reading\nL47: \nL48: cite16†View all L49: \nL50: cite17†Operation “Date Bait”: AI-enabled scam targeting loveseekers SafetyFeb 1, 2026 L51: \nL52: cite18†Operation “False Witness”: Fake recovery service impersonating authorities SafetyFeb 1, 2026 L53: \nL54: cite19†Silver lining playbook: Likely China-origin activity targeting US persons SafetyFeb 1, 2026 L55: \nL56: Research\nL57: \nL58:   * cite20†Research Index L59: 